=E2=80=9CFearmongering, confrontation and vicious competition will only dis=
rupt the process of global AI governance,=E2=80=9D China=E2=80=99s foreign =
ministry said last week, in a statement that seemingly rejected the possibi=
lity of any US-led slowdown.

Any AI slowdown would need buy-in from the US and China, but the rival nati=
ons see each other as the strongest argument against such a pause. Trump wa=
nts AI to go full steam ahead, fortified in that conviction by Silicon Vall=
ey leaders who=E2=80=99ve long been wary of Chinese ambitions. Even Dario A=
modei, while calling for a global coordinated pause, repeatedly called Chin=
a a threat.

China, meanwhile, sees appeals for a slowdown as a way for the US to solidi=
fy American companies=E2=80=99 lead. Would you accept a hobbling proposal f=
rom your first-place opponent?

That doesn=E2=80=99t mean Beijing shares none of Anthropic=E2=80=99s concer=
ns.

Last week, China released the third iteration of its AI safety governance f=
ramework. Included in the updates are risks posed by agents, cybersecurity =
threats from frontier models and the issue of self-improving AI. Amodei has=
, likewise, highlighted risks on all three fronts.

